    afterward is a local change.
  - [1mWord boundary before maxLength[0m — reads better for prose. Needs a fallback to the hard cut anyway, for the case where
    the first word is already longer than the budget ([38;5;153m"supercalifragilistic..."[39m has no boundary to find). So it is [3mhard[0m
    [3mcut plus one step[0m, not a different algorithm — about 4 extra lines. Output length becomes variable but stays within
    the bound.

  [1mEllipsis accounting[0m (this is the one with a tail)

  - [1m[38;5;153m'...'[39m counted inside maxLength[0m — guarantees [38;5;153mresult.length <= maxLength[39m, always. That guarantee is the whole reason a
    caller passes a max: a table column, a terminal width, a database field. Cheap now, painful to retrofit — flipping
    it later silently changes every existing caller's output width.
  - [1m[38;5;153m'...'[39m appended beyond maxLength[0m — slightly simpler to read in the source, but output can be [38;5;153mmaxLength + 3[39m, so the
    option no longer bounds anything and callers have to subtract 3 themselves.

  One consequence worth naming before you pick: to hold a real length guarantee, truncate has to run [1mlast[0m in the option
  chain — after [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m — otherwise appending a suffix blows past the bound. That means a long [38;5;153msuffix[39m can get
  truncated away. The alternative (truncate before prefix/suffix, matching the current positional order) keeps the
  suffix intact but makes [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m a bound on the middle of the string rather than on what you get back.

  [1mMy recommendation: word boundary with hard-cut fallback, ellipsis counted inside [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, applied last.[0m The
  word-boundary work is small and mostly code you'd write anyway for the fallback, and the counted ellipsis is the only
  version where [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m means what the name says. I'd take the truncated-suffix consequence over a bound that doesn't
  bind.

